<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Galich-Mersky</h1><p class="st-meta">1246 CE – 1363 CE (approx.) · capital Galich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An appanage around Galich in the north-east of Rus', whose princes later fought Moscow in its great dynastic civil war.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galich-kostroma-oblast">Galich</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-galich">Lake Galich</a> in the old lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merya">Finnic Merya</a>, was a town of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal</a> region known for its salt springs. In 1246 it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of Konstantin, son of Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</a>, and his descendants held it for over a century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was a remote northern land of forests and salt workings, with little recorded about its government. Its princes were minor figures in the struggles of the grand princes of Vladimir and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galich-kostroma-oblast">Galich</a> often served as a refuge from the turmoil further south.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1363 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> expelled its last independent prince and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galich-kostroma-oblast">Galich</a>. It was later granted to Donskoy's son Yuri, whose line fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II</a> for the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in the civil war of the 1420s to 1450s.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1246 CE – 1363 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.50%;width:2.33%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1246 CE – 1363 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Galich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Konstantin Yaroslavich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Galich-Kostroma, Galich Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1304&amp;focus=principality-of-galich-mersky" aria-label="Principality of Galich-Mersky on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5580 208 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Galich-Mersky lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5900 464L5878 523M5900 464L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5878 523h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5900 464h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5900 464h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Galich. <a href="/atlas?year=1304&amp;focus=principality-of-galich-mersky">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
